{"id":"f22e4912-007b-4ed9-a857-8f0903c35a64","object":"chat.completion","created":1785023133,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    required_symbols_set = set(required_symbols)\\n\\n    node_dict = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    parent_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        parent_map[nid] = parent\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = node['id']\\n            break\\n\\n    retained_set = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    required_counts = {}\\n    for nid, node in node_dict.items():\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols_set:\\n            required_counts[sym] = required_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_dict.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n\\n    removed_order = []\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained_set:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols_set:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for child_id in children_map.get(nid, []):\\n            if child_id in retained_set:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n\\n        def get_subtree(node_id):\\n            subtree = set()\\n            stack = [node_id]\\n            while stack:\\n                current = stack.pop()\\n                if current in retained_set and current not in subtree:\\n                    subtree.add(current)\\n                    for child in children_map.get(current, []):\\n                        stack.append(child)\\n            return subtree\\n\\n        subtree_retained = get_subtree(nid)\\n        can_remove = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols_set:\\n            count_in_subtree = sum(1 for n in subtree_retained if node_dict[n]['symbol'] == sym)\\n            if count_in_subtree > 0 and count_in_subtree >= required_counts.get(sym, 0):\\n                can_remove = False\\n                break\\n        if not can_remove:\\n            continue\\n\\n        for rem_id in subtree_retained:\\n            retained_set.discard(rem_id)\\n            sym = node_dict[rem_id]['symbol']\\n            if sym in required_symbols_set:\\n                required_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained_set)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" based on Perses syntax-guided reduction. The input payload has nodes (each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols. The objective is to remove nodes subject to constraints: never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children. Tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented. Use depth (shallower? actually \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So process deeper nodes first? Actually \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That means we should consider nodes at greater depth first (i.e., prioritize removal of deeper nodes). Then tie-break by node ID (stable). Return removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted).\n\nThe algorithm: \n- Build a map from id to node.\n- Build children map: parent -> list of children.\n- Determine set of required symbols from required_symbols.\n- Identify nodes that are not removable (false) or are root, required symbol. But also removable nodes may be removed only if all required symbols remain after removal. However we must also ensure we never remove a node that has retained children? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if we remove a node, its children become orphaned? Or maybe removal means the node is deleted and its children are reattached to its parent? In Perses-style reduction, removing a node means deleting the subtree? Actually typical grammar-guided reduction: you can delete a node (removing it and its subtree) or replace with a child. But the description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". However the output fields are removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. So we are selecting which nodes to remove. The constraints: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That implies that we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. Because if we remove a node, its children are also removed (or maybe they become children of the grandparent? But the constraint says \"node with retained children\" so you can't remove a node that still has children that are retained. So removal cascades: if you remove a node, its children are also removed. Therefore we need to decide a set of nodes to remove such that no removed node has a retained descendant. So we can only remove nodes that form a \"pruned\" subtree. The order: deeper nodes first. So we should process nodes in decreasing depth, and for each node if it's removable, not a required symbol, not root, and if removing it still leaves all required symbols represented (i.e., the required symbols appear in the set of retained nodes), then we remove it. When we remove a node, we must also remove all its descendants (since they are implicitly removed). So we need to track which nodes are removed.\n\nSimplify: We have a list of nodes with ids. We'll initially consider all nodes as retained. Then we'll attempt to remove nodes in order (deeper first, tie by id). For each node candidate:\n- Check if it is already removed (skip).\n- Check if its 'removable' property is true.\n- Check if its symbol is in required_symbols? Actually never remove a required symbol, but the field is \"removable\". So required_symbols are symbols that must be present. So nodes with symbols in required_symbols must not be removed regardless of removable flag? The constraint says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we should check if the node's symbol is in required_symbols. Also root's id is \"root\" presumably. So we can't remove root.\n- Check if any of its children are retained (i.e., not removed). If any child is still retained, then we cannot remove this node because it would have retained children.\n- And we need to check if after removing this node (and its entire subtree), all required symbols are still present among the retained nodes. That means the set of symbols from retained nodes must still contain all required_symbols. Since removing a node removes its subtree, we need to check that the required symbols present in the subtree of this node (including the node itself) are not the only occurrences? Actually we need to ensure that after removal, every required symbol still appears in at least one retained node. So we need to compute for each required symbol, the set of nodes that have that symbol. If the node being removed contains a required symbol, and it's the only node with that symbol, then removal would make that symbol missing. So we must keep at least one node per required symbol. Also note that multiple nodes can have the same symbol. So we need to check that after removal of the subtree, each required symbol still has at least one retained node (not in subtree). This is a global condition.\n\nSo algorithm:\n- Build set of required symbols set.\n- Build a mapping from symbol to list of node ids (or count). We'll need to track current retained counts.\n- We'll process nodes sorted by depth descending, then id ascending (for tie). For each node:\n  - If node is root or its symbol in required_symbols (or removable false? Actually removable false means cannot be removed). But we also have \"removable\" property; only nodes with removable=true can be removed. So skip if not removable.\n  - Already removed? skip.\n  - Check if any child (descendant directly? Only immediate children? The constraint says \"node with retained children\". I think it means direct children? Because if a child is removed, it's not retained. So we check immediate children that are still retained (not removed). If any child retained, cannot remove.\n  - Check if removal of this node (and its subtree) would cause any required symbol to become missing. That is, for each required symbol, if the only retained nodes with that symbol are in the subtree of this node, then cannot remove.\n  - If all ok, then we remove this node and all its descendants. Mark them removed. Update counts.\n\nWe need to maintain for each required symbol the current retained node count (or set of ids). We can initially count for each symbol the number of nodes (all nodes). As we remove nodes, we decrement counts for symbols of removed nodes. When considering removal, we need to check if the subtree contains all remaining nodes for any required symbol. So for a given node, we need to know the set of required symbols that appear in its subtree, and the count of each in the subtree. Then for each such symbol, if the total remaining count (outside subtree) minus subtree count becomes 0, then removal would cause loss.\n\nSimpler: For each candidate node, compute the set of required symbols that exist in its subtree (including itself). For each such symbol, we need to ensure there is at least one other retained node with that symbol outside the subtree. So we need to know the global counts and the subtree counts.\n\nWe can precompute subtree counts per required symbol for each node. But since removals are sequential and change the retained set, we need to update. Possibly we can recompute on the fly given the list of removed nodes. Since number of nodes is small (in fixtures, 7), O(N^2) is fine.\n\nApproach:\n- Keep a set of removed node ids.\n- Build children map (for each parent, list of child ids).\n- Define function subtree_nodes(node_id, removed_set): recursively collect all nodes in subtree that are not removed? Actually when considering removal, we are about to remove the node, so we consider the current retained subtree under that node (i.e., nodes not already removed). But if some of its descendants are already removed, then they are not in the subtree for this consideration. However, if a descendant is already removed, then this node can still be considered? The constraint \"node with retained children\" only checks immediate children that are retained. So we only need to check immediate children that are not removed. So we don't need to traverse entire subtree for that check. For the symbol check, we need to consider the entire subtree of nodes that would be removed if we remove this node, i.e., all descendants that are still retained (because removing this node would also remove any retained descendants, as removal cascades). But if some descendants are already removed, they are already gone, so removing this node does not affect them. So we need to compute the set of nodes that would be removed: this node plus all retained nodes that are its descendants (i.e., nodes whose ancestor chain from node is all retained? Actually if some descendant is already removed, then that descendant's subtree might still have retained nodes? But if a descendant is removed, its children are also removed? In our procedure, when we remove a node, we remove its entire descendant subtree (all nodes that have it as ancestor). So if we have a situation where a child is already removed, then its descendants are also removed. So for a node, its retained descendants are exactly those descendants that are not removed. So we need to compute all nodes in the subtree of this node that are still retained. That set is the set that would be removed along with this node. Then check if any of those nodes have required symbols that are not present elsewhere.\n\nSo for each candidate node, we need to compute the set of required symbols present in the retained subtree of that node. And ensure that for each such symbol, there is at least one retained node outside this subtree that also has that symbol.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n2. Build node_dict: id -> node (including all fields).\n3. Build parent_map: id -> parent id (or None for root). Also children_map: parent_id -> list of child ids (preserving order).\n4. Initial retained_set = set of all node ids (initially all).\n5. Compute initial required_symbol_counts: dict symbol -> count (over all nodes). Since all nodes retained initially.\n6. Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending. (depth is provided in node, but root depth 0, others >0.)\n7. Process sorted nodes:\n   - if node_id not in retained_set, skip.\n   - node = node_dict[node_id]\n   - if not node['removable']: skip\n   - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols_set: skip (required symbol)\n   - if node_id == 'root': skip (root)\n   - Check immediate children: for each child in children_map.get(node_id, []), if child in retained_set, then cannot remove (node has retained child). Skip.\n   - Compute subtree_retained_ids: traverse starting from node_id, collect all ids that are in retained_set and are descendants (including node itself). Use recursion or stack.\n   - For each required symbol (in required_symbols_set), compute count_in_subtree = number of nodes in subtree_retained_ids that have that symbol.\n   - If for any required symbol, count_in_subtree == required_symbol_counts[symbol] (i.e., all retained occurrences are in this subtree), then removal would make symbol missing -> skip.\n   - Otherwise, we can remove. Remove all nodes in subtree_retained_ids from retained_set. Update required_symbol_counts by subtracting for each symbol the count_in_subtree. Add node_id to removed_node_ids list (append to removal order). (But we need to record which nodes are removed; we can also add all subtree ids to removed_set). Actually the output expects removed_node_ids ordered by removal. So we add node_id only, not the entire subtree? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It says \"removed_node_ids\" - presumably the order in which nodes were removed. Since removal of a node removes its entire subtree, do we list all removed nodes? Or just the node that was explicitly removed? The output schema: \"removed_node_ids\": array of strings. The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The removal order likely means the sequence of node IDs that were selected for removal (the trigger nodes). The retained node IDs are sorted. The removed_node_ids should be the list of IDs that were removed, in the order they were removed. But if removal of a node also removes its descendants, are those descendants listed as removed? Probably not, because the algorithm only selects the topmost node to remove; the descendants are implicitly removed. In Perses, you remove a node and its entire subtree, but the removed_node_ids typically list the nodes that were removed as part of that operation. However the fixture outputs? We don't have expected outputs. The instruction: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" I think removal order refers to the order in which nodes are eliminated (the explicit removals). But it's ambiguous. Let's look at typical problems: The output schema has removed_node_ids (array) and retained_node_ids (array). Both required. The retained_node_ids are sorted (presumably lexicographically). The removed_node_ids are likely the list of node ids that were removed, in the order they were removed. Since removal removes a node and its subtree, we need to list only the node that was chosen for removal (the root of the removed subtree). Because listing all descendants would be redundant and not match the typical \"removal order\" of decisions. The description says \"the actual removal order\" - that suggests the sequence of nodes that the algorithm decided to remove. So we will add the node_id to removed_node_ids when we decide to remove it. But note that later removals may include nodes that are descendants of previously removed nodes? Those descendants are already removed, so we skip.\n\nBut consider if a node is removed, its descendants are removed, so they will not appear later. So we only add the top-level removal nodes.\n\nThus:\n- removed_node_ids will be a list of node ids in the order we removed them.\n- retained_node_ids will be sorted set of all node ids not removed (i.e., remaining after all removals).\n\nWe also need to handle the case where after removals, some nodes may have parents that are removed? That's fine.\n\nBut careful: When we remove a node, we also effectively remove its retained descendants. But those descendants might have been candidates earlier? Since we process in decreasing depth, deeper nodes are processed first. So if we remove a node, its descendants are already processed? Actually deeper nodes are processed first. So if we remove a node, its descendants (which are deeper) would have already been considered. But if they were considered and not removed (maybe because they weren't removable or required), then later when we consider the parent, we might remove it, which would then remove those descendants. That is allowed because deeper nodes were considered first, but they were not removed due to constraints. Later the parent removal may be allowed and removes them. So the order is consistent.\n\nSo our algorithm processes in decreasing depth, so deeper nodes are considered first. For each node, if it is not removable, we skip. If it is removable and satisfies conditions, we remove it. This is correct.\n\nEdge case: What if a node is removable but removing it would cause required symbol loss? Then we skip it. Later, when we consider a shallower node, removing that shallower node might also remove this node (as descendant) but that could cause required symbol loss in the same way. So it will also be skipped. So that's fine.\n\nNow implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nMake sure to import nothing? Use only standard library.\n\nReturn a dict with keys removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids (list of strings).\n\nTest with given fixtures? We can mentally test with first payload.\n\nNodes:\nroot (depth0, removable false, symbol root) required symbol.\nn1 (depth1, removable false, symbol s1) required symbol.\nn2 (depth1, removable true, symbol s2)\nn3 (depth1, removable false, symbol s0)\nn4 (depth2, removable true, symbol s1, parent n2) - but s1 is required, so cannot remove n4 because its symbol is required? Actually required_symbols includes s1, so any node with symbol s1 cannot be removed. So n4 is not removable even though removable field true. So skip.\nn5 (depth2, removable false, symbol s2, parent n3) - not removable.\nn6 (depth2, removable true, symbol s0, parent n1) - not required, removable true.\n\nProcess depths: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. n4 skip (required symbol). n5 skip (not removable). n6: check children? None. Check symbol s0: required_symbols = {root, s1}. s0 not required. Check if removal of n6 would cause any required symbol missing: subtree includes n6 only, with symbol s0. Required symbols are root and s1. They are present in other retained nodes. So we can remove n6. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"]. Remove n6 from retained_set, update counts.\n\nNext depth1 nodes: n1 (skip, removable false), n2 (removable true, symbol s2 not required), n3 (removable false). For n2: check children: n4 is child, still retained? n4 is retained (symbol s1 required, so cannot remove n4 but it's still retained). So n2 has retained child n4. So cannot remove n2. For n3: removable false, skip.\n\ndepth0 root: skip.\n\nFinal retained: all except n6. So retained_node_ids sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. removed_node_ids: [\"n6\"].\n\nBut check if any other removal possible? n2 cannot because child n4 retained. Could we remove n4? n4 removable true but symbol s1 required, so no. So output matches expectation? Possibly.\n\nSecond fixture: same structure, but different utilities. But algorithm doesn't use utility. So same outcome? Actually n6 removable true, n4 required, n2 with child n4. So likely same.\n\nThird fixture: similar. So outputs for these three would be identical except node ids are same? The node ids are fixed (\"root\",\"n1\",...,\"n6\"). So for all three, removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"], retained as above.\n\nBut the problem might have additional considerations like replacement candidates? The description mentions \"deletion and replacement candidates\". But the output schema only has removed and retained. Maybe replacement is not needed? The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\", which typically includes both deletion and replacement. However the output fields suggest only removal decisions. Perhaps the \"removed_node_ids\" are those that are deleted, and \"retained_node_ids\" are those that survive. Replacement might be a different operation? But in Perses, replacement is another type of reduction. The constraints mention \"grammar_valid\", \"interestingness_preserved\", etc. But the given fixtures don't show any alternative candidates. The description: \"Stay inside this mechanism boundary: Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So we are only implementing the reduction logic based on given tree and symbols. The required_symbols act as the interestingness oracle? \"preserving an interestingness oracle\" - required_symbols are the symbols that must be present, so interestingness is \"does the tree contain all required symbols?\" So we are reducing by removing nodes while preserving that property.\n\nThus our algorithm seems correct.\n\nOne more detail: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The root is also a required symbol? In fixtures root symbol is \"root\" which is in required_symbols, so it's covered. So we can just check required symbols set including root.\n\nAlso \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That is the symbol check.\n\n\"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Sorted by depth descending, then id ascending.\n\n\"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted lexicographically (string sort). Removed_node_ids in removal order (order we added).\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use a stack or recursion for subtree traversal. Since depth is small, recursion fine. But avoid recursion limit? Small.\n- For efficiency, we can precompute ancestors? Not needed.\n\nWe'll write solve(case) that takes payload dict. Extract nodes and required_symbols.\n\nBuild structures.\n\nImplement.\n\nLet's write the code in Python and embed it in JSON.\n\nNote: The JSON output must be escaped. We'll produce a single JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source as a string. We must escape newlines, backslashes, and quotes.\n\nWe'll write the Python code with triple quotes? No, we need to embed as JSON string. We'll write it as a normal string and then convert to JSON using json.dumps. Since we are generating the response manually, we need to escape correctly. We'll produce the Python source as a string with proper escaping.\n\nWe'll use the following Python code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    required_symbols_set = set(required_symbols)\n    \n    # Build maps\n    node_dict = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    children_map = {}\n    parent_map = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        parent = node['parent']\n        parent_map[nid] = parent\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n    # Root is node with parent None\n    root_id = None\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = node['id']\n            break\n    \n    retained_set = set(node_dict.keys())\n    # Count required symbols\n    required_counts = {}\n    for nid, node in node_dict.items():\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols_set:\n            required_counts[sym] = required_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    \n    # Sort nodes: depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_dict.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    removed_order = []\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid not in retained_set:\n            continue\n        # Check removable\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbol\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols_set:\n            continue\n        # Check root (though root's symbol is required, but double-check)\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        # Check immediate retained children\n        has_retained_child = False\n        for child_id in children_map.get(nid, []):\n            if child_id in retained_set:\n                has_retained_child = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        \n        # Compute subtree retained set\n        def get_subtree(node_id):\n            subtree = set()\n            stack = [node_id]\n            while stack:\n                current = stack.pop()\n                if current in retained_set and current not in subtree:\n                    subtree.add(current)\n                    for child in children_map.get(current, []):\n                        stack.append(child)\n            return subtree\n        \n        subtree_retained = get_subtree(nid)\n        # Check required symbols in subtree\n        can_remove = True\n        for sym in required_symbols_set:\n            count_in_subtree = sum(1 for n in subtree_retained if node_dict[n]['symbol'] == sym)\n            if count_in_subtree > 0 and count_in_subtree >= required_counts.get(sym, 0):\n                can_remove = False\n                break\n        \n        if not can_remove:\n            continue\n        \n        # Perform removal\n        for rem_id in subtree_retained:\n            retained_set.discard(rem_id)\n            sym = node_dict[rem_id]['symbol']\n            if sym in required_symbols_set:\n                required_counts[sym] -= 1\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained_set)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n```\n\nWe'll need to be careful about variable name conflicts. Also make sure to handle case where children_map may not have entry for a node with no children (use .get(nid, [])).\n\nTest with given fixture: expected removed_order = [\"n6\"], retained_sorted = [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. Works.\n\nNow embed into JSON.\n\nWe must produce exactly:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"python source escaped\"}\n\nMake sure to escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". We'll write the source with proper indentation but we can also compress to one line to avoid escaping multiline? But easier to write with newlines and escape. We'll write the code as a multiline string and then replace newlines with \\n, quotes with \\\", etc. Since we are doing manually, we can construct the string with explicit escapes.\n\nAlternatively, we can write the Python code in a single line with semicolons, but that's less readable. The instruction doesn't forbid newlines in the source, but JSON requires escaping. So we can include newlines as \\n.\n\nI'll produce the JSON response with the implementation string containing the Python code with proper escaping.\n\nI'll write the code as a string and then manually escape. For safety, I'll write it in a text editor and then replace. But since I'm generating response here, I'll ensure it's valid.\n\nLet's do it step by step:\n\n- Start with `{\"implementation\": \"`\n- Then the Python code with:\n  - `def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    required_symbols_set = set(required_symbols)\\n\\n    node_dict = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    parent_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        parent_map[nid] = parent\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = node['id']\\n            break\\n\\n    retained_set = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    required_counts = {}\\n    for nid, node in node_dict.items():\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols_set:\\n            required_counts[sym] = required_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(node_dict.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n\\n    removed_order = []\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained_set:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols_set:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for child_id in children_map.get(nid, []):\\n            if child_id in retained_set:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n\\n        def get_subtree(node_id):\\n            subtree = set()\\n            stack = [node_id]\\n            while stack:\\n                current = stack.pop()\\n                if current in retained_set and current not in subtree:\\n                    subtree.add(current)\\n                    for child in children_map.get(current, []):\\n                        stack.append(child)\\n            return subtree\\n\\n        subtree_retained = get_subtree(nid)\\n        can_remove = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols_set:\\n            count_in_subtree = sum(1 for n in subtree_retained if node_dict[n]['symbol'] == sym)\\n            if count_in_subtree > 0 and count_in_subtree >= required_counts.get(sym, 0):\\n                can_remove = False\\n                break\\n        if not can_remove:\\n            continue\\n\\n        for rem_id in subtree_retained:\\n            retained_set.discard(rem_id)\\n            sym = node_dict[rem_id]['symbol']\\n            if sym in required_symbols_set:\\n                required_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained_set)\\n    return {\\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\\n    }`\n\nThen escape: replace all backslashes with \\\\\\\\, then all double quotes with \\\", then newlines with \\n.\n\nWe need to be careful with nested quotes inside the code. For example, case['nodes'] uses single quotes, that's fine because we are using double quotes for JSON string. But we have double quotes in the return dict. Those need to be escaped. So we replace \" with \\\". Also in the string we have backslashes? Not really, except maybe in esca